body:
Theme CSS media references: a theme's `theme.css` — and every CSS it `@import`s, wherever in the resolved library that CSS lives (its own theme directory, another theme's directory, or `media/`) — may reference a servable library file with both `url()` and `@import`, in three forms: an unprefixed relative path, the reserved `media:` prefix, or the reserved `theme:<name>/` prefix. This lets several themes share one set of brand assets (fonts, backgrounds, logos, stylesheets) from a single library `media/` copy and lets a theme build on another, instead of duplicating the files and their `@font-face` CSS into every `themes/<name>/` directory or moving the styling into layouts.

Reference forms and confinement:
- An unprefixed relative reference resolves from the referencing file's own location and must stay inside that file's own tree: a stylesheet in a theme directory stays inside that theme directory (`themes/<name>/`), and a stylesheet imported through the `media:` prefix stays inside `media/`. A relative `..` that leaves that tree is an error — e.g. `url('../other/x.woff2')` or `url('../../media/x')` from `themes/a/theme.css`, or `url('../slides/x/layout.html.tmpl')` — reported with the offending CSS file and line. A relative reference never reaches across trees (another theme, `media/` from a theme, or a non-servable library entry such as `slides/`, `sections/`, `library.yaml` or `AGENTS.md`): those files are either not served at all or are reachable only through a reserved prefix, and a relative path that would reach them is rejected even though those files exist.
- The reserved `media:` prefix references the resolved library's shared `media/` tree, e.g. `url('media:fonts/Inter-Regular.woff2')`. The path after the prefix is relative to the resolved library's `media/` (the SAME base as the layout `media` helper) and stays within it.
- The reserved `theme:<name>/` prefix references another theme's directory, e.g. `url('theme:default/theme.css')`. The path after the prefix is relative to `themes/<name>/` and stays within it; an unknown `<name>` is an error.
Cross-tree reach therefore happens ONLY through `media:` / `theme:<name>/`: an unprefixed relative reference cannot leave its own tree, and no form may reference `slides/`, `sections/`, `library.yaml` or `AGENTS.md` (global.constraint.template-library-shareable). The `media:` and `theme:` prefixes are valid for both `url()` and `@import`, and every target stays inside the library root.

Guards (all forms): the target file exists — validated at load time (templates-dir-validation step 6) — and every reference is rewritten at serve time to the served URL (the same URL the layout `media` helper returns, so the browser fetches it from the resolved library). A stylesheet imported through `media:` is served and rewritten from the `media/` route; one imported through `theme:<name>/` is served and rewritten from that theme's route. A missing file, an absolute path, a `..` leaving the referencing file's own tree, any other scheme (`http:`, `https:`, `data:`, `//`, …), an unknown `theme:<name>`, or a reference outside the library is a templates error qualified with the offending CSS file and line (requirements.constraint.media-paths-library-relative). An unprefixed relative reference stays inside its own tree — a theme directory, or `media/` for a stylesheet imported through `media:`; it never reaches another theme, `media/` from a theme, or a non-servable library entry such as `slides/` or `library.yaml`, and a `..` that would do so is rejected. `@import` is followed in both the quoted-string form `@import "x.css";` and the `url()` form `@import url("x.css");`; imports are bounded and cycle-safe across the whole import graph (including a theme↔media cycle and a theme↔theme cycle). A theme's CSS reaches its own theme directory, the shared `media/` tree and another theme's files only through these rules, preserving the relocatable-library property (global.constraint.template-library-shareable). Both embedded authoring guides document the syntax (agent-authoring, library-agent-guide, agent-guide-drift-check).

AC:
- a `media:` `url()` in a theme's `theme.css` naming an existing file under the library's `media/` is served and applied in the deck;
- a relative `url()` in `theme.css` naming an existing file in its own theme directory is served and applied;
- a `theme:<name>/` `url()` or `@import` naming an existing file in another theme's directory is served and applied (a theme may build on another);
- an `@import url('media:css/x.css')` and an `@import url('theme:default/theme.css')` are followed, and an imported stylesheet's own `media:`/`theme:`/relative references are checked and rewritten too;
- a relative `..` reference leaving its own tree is rejected with a templates error naming the offending CSS file and line — e.g. `url('../other/x.woff2')` from `themes/a/theme.css` (cross-tree reach through a relative path), and `url('../../slides/x/layout.html.tmpl')` or `url('../../library.yaml')` from `themes/a/theme.css` (non-servable entries) — none resolves, even though the target files exist;
- a `media:` reference to a missing file, a `theme:<name>/` reference to an unknown theme or to a missing file, an absolute path, another scheme, and a reference outside the library are each rejected with a templates error naming the CSS file and line;
- an `@import` cycle spanning a theme and `media/`, or two themes, is rejected.
- both embedded guides document both prefixes, drop the rule that a theme reaches only its own directory and the rule that `media:` is forbidden on `@import`, and removing the `media:` or the `theme:` prefix token from BOTH guides fails the agent-guide drift self-test (agent-guide-drift-check).
